<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kadambas of Goa</h1><p class="st-meta">960 CE – 1345 CE (approx.) · capital Gopakapattana</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Kadamba dynasty that ruled Goa for nearly four centuries, made rich by Arab trade through the port of Gopakapattana.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kadamba-dynasty">Kadambas</a> claimed descent from the earlier Kadamba dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banavasi">Banavasi</a>. Shashthadeva I established them in the Goa region around 960, at first as feudatories of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire</a>, with the capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandor">Chandrapura</a>, modern Chandor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayakeshi-i">Jayakeshi I</a> moved the capital to the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa-velha">Gopakapattana</a> on the Zuari river, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> Velha, which became a busy harbour trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>; the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kadamba-dynasty">Kadambas</a> welcomed Arab merchants and allowed a mosque to be built. They issued gold coins and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silahara-dynasty">Silaharas</a> for control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konkan-coast">Konkan coast</a>. The Mahadeva temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tambdi-surla-mahadeva-temple">Tambdi Surla</a>, built of basalt, survives from their period.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 13th century the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kadamba-dynasty">Kadambas</a> came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Yadavas</a>, and in the 14th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> was raided by the armies of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. Around 1345 their rule collapsed, and Goa later passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kadambas-of-goa.jpg" alt="Kadambas of Goa" width="320" height="158"><figcaption>Image: Nijgoykar · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kadamba.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>960 CE – 1345 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gopakapattana</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Shashthadeva II, Jayakeshi I, Shivachitta Paramadi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit, Kannada</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Goa Kadambas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1152&amp;focus=kadambas-of-goa" aria-label="Kadambas of Goa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6828 1329 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kadambas of Goa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7088 1770h0M7060 1881h0M7018 1385h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7148 1585h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7148 1585h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day India. <a href="/atlas?year=1152&amp;focus=kadambas-of-goa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

